        "Medical graduates are expected to recognise and initiate management of acutely unwell patients from their first day of internship. Simulation-based education allows deliberate practice of these skills without risk to patients. A meta-analysis of 609 studies found that technology-enhanced simulation, compared with no intervention, was associated with large effects on knowledge, skills and behaviours and moderate effects on patient-related outcomes [2].",
        "However, the comparison that matters for curriculum planners is not simulation versus nothing, but simulation versus an active, cheaper alternative. A meta-analytic review found that simulation-based education with deliberate practice outperformed traditional clinical education for specific clinical skills (pooled effect size 0.71) [1], although the number of eligible studies was small. Resuscitation education statements recommend structured debriefing, feedback and spaced practice [3, 4].",
